  forms inherit.

  [1mA. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m owns the write.[0m Extract [38;5;153mlogin()[39m and [38;5;153mvalidateForm()[39m into [38;5;153msrc/auth.js[39m; add [38;5;153msrc/session.js[39m owning
  [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m. On success, [38;5;153mlogin()[39m calls [38;5;153msession.set(...)[39m itself and resolves the record. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m shrinks to DOM
  wiring. A future form does [38;5;153mawait login(u, p)[39m and the identity is simply there.
  [3mWins:[0m exactly one write path, so a caller cannot forget to persist and the stored [38;5;153musername[39m can never drift from the
  one that authenticated. [3mCosts:[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is no longer pure — testing it means pointing [38;5;153msession.js[39m at a fake storage.

  [1mB. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m stays pure; the caller persists.[0m [38;5;153mlogin()[39m resolves [38;5;153m{ success, userId, username, loginAt }[39m and touches
  nothing; the submit handler calls [38;5;153msession.set(result)[39m. [3mWins:[0m auth and storage stay independent, and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is
  testable with no storage at all. [3mCosts:[0m the write path multiplies with every new form, and "I called login but the
  user isn't logged in" becomes a real bug class — precisely as more forms arrive.

  [1mC. A [38;5;153msignIn()[39m facade.[0m [38;5;153msession.js[39m exposes [38;5;153msignIn(username, password)[39m wrapping both; [38;5;153mlogin()[39m becomes internal. [3mWins:[0m
  smallest public surface — one import, one verb. [3mCosts:[0m a third layer over a stub, and auth and storage fuse into one
  module, so swapping either drags the other.

  [1mMy recommendation: A.[0m You told me more forms are coming, which is the exact condition that turns B's "remember to
  persist" into a recurring bug and makes C's fused module harder to unpick. A keeps one write path and one obvious
  module boundary, and its only real cost — an injectable storage for tests — is something we want anyway.

  Two things I'm deliberately leaving out unless you say otherwise: no subscribe/observer mechanism on the store
  (nothing needs to react to login state yet — YAGNI), and no session expiry ([38;5;153mloginAt[39m makes it possible later without a
  migration).
